$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):CAT(1):CAS_RN
$DATUM 7440-05-3
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):SOL(1):CAS_RN
$DATUM 68-12-2
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Pd Catalysed Ligand-Free Heck Reaction. A variety of aryl halides and
alkenes were used to examine the effectiveness of the monolithic reactor
to promote the Heck cross-coupling reaction in DMF in the presence of
triethylamine. In each reaction, an aryl halide (0.2 mmol), an alkene
(0.3 mmol), and triethylamine (0.3 mmol) were dissolved in DMF (1.0 mL),
and this mixture was introduced onto the monolithic nanoparticular Pd
reactor column using either manual loop injection or an automated
injection module19 with DMF as the system solvent pumped at 0.05 mL/min
throughout (Figure 3). The reactor column was maintained at 130 °C using
a convective heating device. The output from the flow reactor was
collected for 2.0 h, either manually or by automated fraction collection,
and then the solvent was evaporated under high vacuum and the residue
dissolved in dichloromethane and was washed with water. Removal of the
dichloromethane afforded the isolated products which were characterised
by LC-MS and NMR analysis (Table 2).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM solid-supported catalyst, Heck reaction, Pd(0) nanoparticles used,
copolymer of vinyl benzyl chloride-divinyl benzene (monolith) used,
flow-through reactor system used
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Continuous Flow Ligand-Free Heck Reactions Using Monolithic Pd [0]
Nanoparticles
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Nikbin, Nikzad; Ladlow, Mark; Ley, Steven V.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Process Research & Development (2007), 11(3), 458-462

